A linked list is a collection of nodes connected together. Each node stores two things: a value (the actual data) and a next reference (which points to the next node).

Example:

10 → 20 → 30 → 40 → None

Creating a Node in Python

class Node:
    def __init__(self, value):
        self.value = value
        self.next = None

Creating a node:

node1 = Node(10)

This gives us a node containing 10, with next initially set to None.

 Creating a Complete Linked List

Create four nodes:

node1 = Node(10)
node2 = Node(20)
node3 = Node(30)
node4 = Node(40)

Connect them:

node1.next = node2
node2.next = node3
node3.next = node4

Result:

10 → 20 → 30 → 40 → None
Understanding .value and .next

node.value → gets the value stored in the node

node.next → gets the next node

For example, if node contains 10 and points to 20, node.value is 10 and node.next refers to the 20 node.
